{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-5","id":"msg_bdrk_jyyzhbb7qpqbbqyrablyfarztsggbodazzozsjjkz6gjq6iwi4la","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"tool_use","id":"toolu_bdrk_01ScoE5ob2hqUpELCksAWm9y","name":"Write","input":{"file_path":"/local/home/scheschb/.harvest/work/harvest-work-VVQnEx/translation/ERRORS.md","content":"# ERRORS.md — error-surface table\n\nDerived mechanically from `c_src/src/lib.c` by grepping for **every** place the\ncode rejects input or aborts:\n\n```\ngrep -n 'cp_error_reason =\\|return 0;\\|return NULL\\|return 0\\b\\|assert(' c_src/src/lib.c\n```\n\nThe C library has no error enum.  It signals failure three ways:\n\n* `cp_inflate` returns `0` (success `1`) and leaves a diagnostic in the global\n  `const char *cp_error_reason`;\n* internal `static` helpers return `0`;\n* a failed `assert()` (the CMake build defines no `NDEBUG`, so asserts are\n  **live**) prints\n  `\"<prog>: <abs-path>/lib.c:<line>: <func>: Assertion `<expr>' failed.\"` and\n  raises `SIGABRT`.\n\n`convert_pix` contains **no** validation whatsoever — it has no error surface\n(see rows 22–23).\n\n## Table\n\nLegend for the \"test\" column: name of the differential test that covers the row\n(`tests/errors.rs` unless stated otherwise).  Abort rows are checked by\nre-executing the test binary as a child process and comparing\n`(exit status, signal, stderr)` between the C and the Rust `.so`\n(`tests/aborts.rs`).\n\n| #  | function | trigger (exact invalid input / condition) | expected C result | [x] | test |\n|----|----------|-------------------------------------------|-------------------|-----|------|\n| 1  | `cp_stored` (line 170) | stored block whose `LEN != (uint16_t)~NLEN` | `cp_inflate` → `0`, `cp_error_reason` = `\"Failed to find LEN and NLEN as complements within stored (uncompressed) stream.\"` | [x] | `err01_stored_len_nlen_mismatch` |\n| 2  | `cp_stored` (line 179) | stored block where `s->bits_left / 8 > LEN`, i.e. **more** input bytes remain than `LEN` (note: the C test is `<=`, the inverse of the intuitive one) | `cp_inflate` → `0`, `cp_error_reason` = `\"Stored block extends beyond end of input stream.\"` | [x] | `err02_stored_extends_beyond` |\n| 3  | `cp_block` (line 254) | literal symbol decoded while `s->out + 1 > s->out_end` (output buffer full / `out_bytes == 0`) | `cp_inflate` → `0`, `cp_error_reason` = `\"Attempted to overwrite out buffer while outputting a symbol.\"` | [x] | `err03_out_full_on_literal` |\n| 4  | `cp_block` (line 273) | length/distance pair whose `s->out - backwards_distance < s->begin` (back-reference before start of output) | `cp_inflate` → `0`, `cp_error_reason` = `\"Attempted to write before out buffer (invalid backwards distance).\"` | [x] | `err04_bad_backwards_distance` |\n| 5  | `cp_block` (line 282) | length/distance pair whose `s->out + length > s->out_end` | `cp_inflate` → `0`, `cp_error_reason` = `\"Attempted to overwrite out buffer while outputting a string.\"` | [x] | `err05_out_full_on_string` |\n| 6  | `cp_inflate` (line 356) | `btype == 3` (bits `11` after BFINAL) | `cp_inflate` → `0`, `cp_error_reason` = `\"Detected unknown block type within input stream.\"` | [x] | `err06_btype_3_unknown_block` |\n| 7  | `cp_unfilter` (line 434) | row-0 filter byte `> 4` | `return 0` | [x] | unreachable: `cp_unfilter` is `static` and never called (not in `nm -D` of either `.so`); documented, no test possible |\n| 8  | `cp_unfilter` (line 468) | row-`y` (`y >= 1`) filter byte `> 4` | `return 0` | [x] | unreachable, same as row 7 |\n| 9  | `cp_chunk` (line 397) | `memcmp(start+4, chunk, 4) != 0`, or `len < minlen`, or `png->p + len + 12 > png->end` | `return NULL` | [x] | unreachable: `static`, never called |\n| 10 | `cp_find` (line 409) | no matching chunk before `png->end` | `return NULL` | [x] | unreachable: `static`, never called |\n| 11 | `cp_ptr` (line 89) | `assert(!(s->bits_left & 7))` — stored block reached with a bit-position that is not byte aligned. Reachable: `cp_stored` aligns using `s->count & 7`, which is only equivalent to bit alignment when `first_bytes == 0`; a mis-aligned `in` pointer (`first_bytes != 0`) leaves `bits_left & 7 != 0`. | `SIGABRT` + `Assertion '!(s->bits_left & 7)' failed.` | [x] | `abort11_cp_ptr_unaligned` |\n| 12 | `cp_peak_bits` (line 98) | `assert(s->word_index <= s->word_count)` | unreachable — the increment happens inside `if (s->word_index < s->word_count)`, so the post-condition always holds | [x] | proven unreachable by inspection; no test |\n| 13 | `cp_consume_bits` (line 109) | `assert(s->count >= num_bits_to_read)` — consume more bits than are buffered. Reachable from `cp_decode` (which does not pre-check) when `key & 0xF` exceeds the buffered bit count, and from `cp_read_bits` for `num_bits_to_read > 32` after `cp_peak_bits` refused to refill. | `SIGABRT` + `Assertion 's->count >= num_bits_to_read' failed.` | [x] | `abort13_consume_more_than_buffered` |\n| 14 | `cp_read_bits` (line 117) | `assert(num_bits_to_read <= 32)` — reachable by tampering `cp_len_extra_bits` / `cp_dist_extra_bits` (both are exported, writable globals) with a value `> 32` | `SIGABRT` + `Assertion 'num_bits_to_read <= 32' failed.` | [x] | `abort14_read_bits_gt_32` |\n| 15 | `cp_read_bits` (line 118) | `assert(num_bits_to_read >= 0)` | unreachable — every argument is either a literal `1,2,3,4,5,7,16`, `s->count & 7`, or a `uint8_t` table entry, all `>= 0` | [x] | proven unreachable by inspection; no test |\n| 16 | `cp_read_bits` (line 119) | `assert(s->bits_left > 0)` — input exhausted, e.g. `cp_inflate(in, 0, ...)`: `bits_left = 0` before the first `cp_read_bits(s, 1)` | `SIGABRT` + `Assertion 's->bits_left > 0' failed.` | [x] | `abort16_in_bytes_zero`, `abort16_truncated_stream` |\n| 17 | `cp_read_bits` (line 120) | `assert(s->count <= 64)` | unreachable — `count` only grows in `cp_peak_bits` by `32` from `count < num_bits_to_read <= 32`, or by `bits_left` when `count < num_bits_to_read`; both keep `count <= 64` for the arguments actually used | [x] | proven unreachable for reachable arguments; no test |\n| 18 | `cp_read_bits` (line 121) | `assert(!cp_would_overflow(s, n))`, i.e. `(bits_left + count) - n < 0` — reading past the end of the stream (`bits_left` nearly exhausted) | `SIGABRT` + `Assertion '!cp_would_overflow(s, num_bits_to_read)' failed.` | [x] | `abort18_would_overflow` |\n| 19 | `cp_build` (line 148) | `assert(len < 16)` — a code-length array entry `>= 16`. Reachable by tampering `cp_fixed_table` (exported, writable) with a value `>= 16` and then decoding a fixed block. | `SIGABRT` + `Assertion 'len < 16' failed.` | [x] | `abort19_code_length_ge_16` |\n| 20 | `cp_decode` (line 211) | `assert((search >> len) == (key >> len))` — the peeked bits do not match any code in the tree (garbage / truncated Huffman data, or an empty tree where `tree[-1]` is read) | `SIGABRT` + `Assertion '(search >> len) == (key >> len)' failed.` | [x] | `abort20_decode_no_match`, `abort20_random_bytes` |\n| 21 | `cp_inflate` | `calloc` returns `NULL` (out of memory) — the C code does **not** check it and dereferences `s` | `SIGSEGV` | [x] | not testable without an allocator interposer; both implementations dereference an unchecked allocation result, documented only |\n\n## Generic FFI boundary cases (required even though not in the C's own checks)\n\n| #  | entry point | trigger | expected C result | [x] | test |\n|----|-------------|---------|-------------------|-----|------|\n| 22 | `convert_pix` | `bpp` outside `{1,2,3,4}`: `0`, `5`, `6`, `255`, `-1`, `INT_MIN`, `INT_MAX` (C `switch` has no `default`, so `dst` is not advanced/written but `src` still advances by `bpp` each pixel) | no output written, function returns normally | [x] | `err22_convert_pix_bad_bpp` (`tests/convert_pix.rs`) |\n| 23 | `convert_pix` | `w <= 0` and/or `h <= 0` (including `INT_MIN`) — loops do not execute, `dst`/`src` untouched; `NULL` `src`/`dst` are then never dereferenced | returns normally, writes nothing | [x] | `err23_convert_pix_nonpositive_and_null` (`tests/convert_pix.rs`) |\n| 24 | `cp_inflate` | `out_bytes == 0` with a non-empty block → row 3; `out_bytes` negative → `out_end < out`, every literal trips row 3 | `0` + row-3 message | [x] | `err24_out_bytes_zero_and_negative` |\n| 25 | `cp_inflate` | `in_bytes == 0` → row 16 abort; `in_bytes` negative → `bits_left < 0` → row 18 abort (`would_overflow`) | `SIGABRT` | [x] | `abort16_in_bytes_zero`, `abort25_in_bytes_negative` |\n| 26 | `cp_inflate` | `in = NULL, in_bytes = 0` | row 16 abort (`bits_left > 0` fails before any dereference) | [x] | `abort26_null_in` |\n| 27 | `cp_inflate` | value one past every documented range: `btype` has only 4 values (2 bits) so `3` is the one-past case (row 6); there are **no** C `enum` parameters in this API, so the \"out-of-range enum\" class collapses onto `bpp` (row 22) and `btype` (row 6) | as rows 6 / 22 | [x] | `err06_btype_3_unknown_block`, `err22_convert_pix_bad_bpp` |\n| 28 | `cp_inflate` | oversized `LEN` in a stored block so that `memcpy` overruns the output buffer (the C performs **no** bound check in `cp_stored`) | returns `1`, writes `LEN` bytes past `out_end` | [x] | `err28_stored_overruns_out` (uses a padded output buffer so the overrun is observable and bounded) |\n| 29 | `cp_stored` | `LEN == 0` (and `NLEN == 0xFFFF`) — zero-length stored block | returns `1`, no bytes written | [x] | `err29_stored_zero_len` |\n"}}],"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":2,"cache_creation_input_tokens":2110,"cache_read_input_tokens":66485,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":2110,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":0},"output_tokens":20,"service_tier":"standard"},"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"a7ff890e-034c-49fc-82fa-f29dc73173cc","uuid":"5191833c-01dd-4f91-96c7-6fd4f011de9b","timestamp":"2026-08-27T07:33:21.374Z"}
