SIGABRT` + `Assertion 'num_bits_to_read <= 32' failed.` | [x] | `abort14_read_bits_gt_32` |","-| 15 | `cp_read_bits` (line 118) | `assert(num_bits_to_read >= 0)` | unreachable — every argument is either a literal `1,2,3,4,5,7,16`, `s->count & 7`, or a `uint8_t` table entry, all `>= 0` | [x] | proven unreachable by inspection; no test |","-| 16 | `cp_read_bits` (line 119) | `assert(s->bits_left > 0)` — input exhausted, e.g. `cp_inflate(in, 0, ...)`: `bits_left = 0` before the first `cp_read_bits(s, 1)` | `SIGABRT` + `Assertion 's->bits_left > 0' failed.` | [x] | `abort16_in_bytes_zero`, `abort16_truncated_stream` |","-| 17 | `cp_read_bits` (line 120) | `assert(s->count <= 64)` | unreachable — `count` only grows in `cp_peak_bits` by `32` from `count < num_bits_to_read <= 32`, or by `bits_left` when `count < num_bits_to_read`; both keep `count <= 64` for the arguments actually used | [x] | proven unreachable for reachable arguments; no test |","-| 18 | `cp_read_bits` (line 121) | `assert(!cp_would_overflow(s, n))`, i.e. `(bits_left + count) - n < 0` — reading past the end of the stream (`bits_left` nearly exhausted) | `SIGABRT` + `Assertion '!cp_would_overflow(s, num_bits_to_read)' failed.` | [x] | `abort18_would_overflow` |","-| 19 | `cp_build` (line 148) | `assert(len < 16)` — a code-length array entry `>= 16`. Reachable by tampering `cp_fixed_table` (exported, writable) with a value `>= 16` and then decoding a fixed block. | `SIGABRT` + `Assertion 'len < 16' failed.` | [x] | `abort19_code_length_ge_16` |","-| 20 | `cp_decode` (line 211) | `assert((search >> len) == (key >> len))` — the peeked bits do not match any code in the tree (garbage / truncated Huffman data, or an empty tree where `tree[-1]` is read) | `SIGABRT` + `Assertion '(search >> len) == (key >> len)' failed.` | [x] | `abort20_decode_no_match`, `abort20_random_bytes` |","-| 21 | `cp_inflate` | `calloc` returns `NULL` (out of memory) — the C code does **not** check it and dereferences `s` | `SIGSEGV` | [x] | not testable without an allocator interposer; both implementations dereference an unchecked allocation result, documented only |","+| 13 | `cp_consume_bits` (line 109) | `assert(s->count >= num_bits_to_read)` — consume more bits than are buffered.  Concrete input: `01 00` (a 2-byte stored block header): after `LEN` is read, `count == 8`, no words remain and `final_word` is spent, so the 16-bit `NLEN` read cannot be satisfied. | `SIGABRT` + `Assertion 's->count >= num_bits_to_read' failed.` | [x] | `aborts::abort13_consume_more_than_buffered`, hit by all three fuzz sweeps |","+| 14 | `cp_read_bits` (line 117) | `assert(num_bits_to_read <= 32)` — reachable by writing `> 32` into the exported, writable `cp_len_extra_bits` / `cp_dist_extra_bits` | `SIGABRT` + `Assertion 'num_bits_to_read <= 32' failed.` | [x] | `aborts::abort14_read_bits_gt_32` |","+| 15 | `cp_read_bits` (line 118) | `assert(num_bits_to_read >= 0)` | unreachable — every argument is a literal `1,2,3,4,5,7,16`, `s->count & 7`, or a `uint8_t` table entry; all `>= 0` | [x] | proven unreachable by inspection; no test |","+| 16 | `cp_read_bits` (line 119) | `assert(s->bits_left > 0)` — input exhausted.  `cp_inflate(in, 0, …)` trips it before the first bit is read. | `SIGABRT` + `Assertion 's->bits_left > 0' failed.` | [x] | `aborts::abort16_in_bytes_zero`, `aborts::abort26_null_in`, `aborts::abort25_in_bytes_negative`, all fuzz sweeps |","+| 17 | `cp_read_bits` (line 120) | `assert(s->count <= 64)` | unreachable — `cp_peak_bits` only refills when `count < num_bits_to_read <= 32`, so `count <= 32 + 32 = 64` afterwards | [x] | proven unreachable for the arguments the code uses; no test |","+| 18 | `cp_read_bits` (line 121) | `assert(!cp_would_overflow(s, n))`, i.e. `(bits_left + count) - n < 0`.  Reachable both from truncated streams and deterministically by setting `cp_len_extra_bits` to `30` and offering a 4-byte fixed block with one match (`bits_left == count == 14`, `28 - 30 < 0`). | `SIGABRT` + `Asse